// Two functions have no equality worth defining, so `==` on them must
// be REJECTED — by the checker, with a source location.
//
// This fixture exists because BOTH compilers accepted it and then
// failed late, differently, and without a span: the interpreter raised
// "cannot compare Closure(0) and Closure(1)" at runtime, and codegen
// refused with "Eq is not supported for Closure([Int], Int) operands".
// One step further out they disagreed outright — a struct with a
// closure FIELD was a runtime error under the interpreter and printed
// `true` under native codegen, for two DIFFERENT closures, because
// struct equality never meaningfully compared that field.

let main (): Unit = {
    let f = |n: Int| n + 1;
    let g = |n: Int| n + 1;
    println((f == g).to_string());
}
